        "A closer look at the session",
        "Give the first five minutes to a warm-up that matches the work: easy cycling or walking, then a few unloaded squats, hip hinges and shoulder movements. The aim is to arrive ready, not to turn the warm-up into a second workout. For each main movement, do two or three working sets. Leave a couple of good repetitions in reserve while learning; the session should finish with effort, not a collapse. Rest long enough to speak in a normal voice before the next set.\n\nOn a squat, one partner might use a goblet hold while the other uses a barbell. On a hinge, one may start with dumbbells and the other with a bar. Both can use the same movement pattern and still have different demands. Write down the variation, load and repetitions for each person separately. If equipment is scarce, alternate sets and keep the weights in clearly different spots.\n\nA simple four-week rhythm is enough: repeat the session twice a week if it fits, add a repetition when the current set feels controlled, and increase the load only after reaching the top of the planned range. If progress stalls, hold steady for another week. A partner can help notice consistency; they should not decide what your body is ready to do."
